      ":question/id": "21520ba3-80e3-5455-9216-9ff33775a80b",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-337099",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x-({x}^{3}+2{y}^{4})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}-\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x})=\\frac{4}{y}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y,\\hspace{2em}Q(x,y)=-{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(y)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{2em}$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant."
    }
